{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"The rule \"a plan review that PASSED but whose attestation failed to persist is RETRYABLE, not\nsuccess\" exists only in the CLI: `_cli/_llm_commands.py:129-171` inspects `result[\"signature\"]`,\ndiscriminates `plan_review_generation_changed` vs `plan_review_sign_aborted` vs transient, and\nreturns exit 11.\n\n`_mcp_llm.py:227-229` returns `rebar.llm.review_plan(...)` raw, with no such inspection. Verified\nend-to-end: an MCP agent sees `verdict: PASS`, proceeds to `claim`, and the claim gate refuses\nbecause the signature the gate consumes was never written.\n\nThe surface most used by autonomous agents has the weaker implementation. The recovery\n(`sign_review`) IS already exposed over MCP — the agent simply is not told to use it. This is a\npresentation gap in the seam, not a missing capability.\n\nBoth proposers converged on extracting the classifier below both front-ends.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] The retryable/cause discrimination lives in one place below CLI and MCP\n- [x] The CLI still maps it to exit 11 with byte-identical stderr\n- [x] The MCP result carries a structured retryable flag and names `sign_review` as the recovery\n- [x] A test covers the passed-but-unsigned state on both surfaces"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786086565884086001,"uuid":"67635182-56a2-477f-a10b-c50c6efc5b5b"}